{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790258093,"nonce":"sKLf8t7GJwiOASLxxXt8zBMUs8w=","title":"Lua method_call: one more method inlined into the top-level chunk costs a fixed ~12 ms","message":"c0dc6a12 (stores into born fields skip the present bit) shrinks Toggle:activate from 33 to 28 HIR instructions. The inliner then fits 7 copies each of activate and value into lua$chunk instead of 6 (the rest stay calls: the per-caller budget runs out), and method_call's elapsed goes from 124 to 137 ms (Mac, interleaved, 5 runs each: 70a41876 0.122 to 0.126 s, c0dc6a12 0.132 to 0.139 s). With n ten times larger the gap stays about 10 ms (70a41876 0.300 s, final 0.310 s, main 0.320 to 0.364 s), so it is a fixed cost, not per call: compiling the larger chunk before the loops reach their fast tier. nbody gains 7% from the same commit.\n\nThe top-level chunk runs each loop once; inlining hot methods into it pays compile latency that a 100000-iteration loop does not earn back. Worth looking at: a budget for inlining into a function that is entered once (the chunk, or a resume helper), or inlining all ten sites or none so the loop body is uniform.","files":null}]}